<h2>The gap between order and release</h2>
<p>When the Delhi High Court or a sessions court grants bail, the operative words are almost always the same: release on a personal bond of a stated amount with one or two sureties of the like amount, to the satisfaction of the trial court or the duty Magistrate. That phrase assigns the actual mechanics to a different judge on a different day. The accused's family must prepare the bond papers, bring the proposed surety with original documents, and persuade the court that the surety is genuine and solvent. Only then does a release warrant travel to the jail. Each step has a failure mode, and each failure costs days.</p>

<h2>The statutory frame</h2>
<p>Chapter XXXV of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023 — the successor to Chapter XXXIII of the CrPC — governs bonds of accused persons and sureties. Its architecture is familiar from the older Code: the bond must state the amount and the obligation to appear; the court fixes the amount with due regard to the circumstances of the case, and the amount must not be excessive; a declaration by sureties discloses how many other bonds they have stood; sureties may seek discharge; and bonds are forfeited through a notice-and-recovery procedure. The BNSS also carries forward the court\'s power to accept a cash deposit in lieu of executing a surety bond — often the fastest lawful route to release where no surety is at hand.</p>

<h2>What verification involves in practice</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Documents</strong> — the surety produces identity and address proof, and material of solvency: property papers, bank statements or salary slips roughly answering the bond amount.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Court scrutiny</strong> — the Magistrate or trial judge examines the surety, confirms the declaration about other pending bonds, and may direct police verification of the address.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Acceptance and warrant</strong> — once satisfied, the court accepts the bonds and issues the release warrant to the jail superintendent.</div>
</div>
<p>Two recurring frictions deserve attention. The first is the demand for a local surety. Convenience of verification is legitimate, but the Supreme Court has long cautioned that insistence on local sureties can make bail illusory for migrants and out-of-State accused; where the condition operates as a denial of release, a modification application should be moved promptly before the granting court. The second is over-valuation: bond amounts far beyond the accused's means offend the statutory command that the amount not be excessive, and are equally open to modification.</p>

<div class="check">
<p>A practical checklist for the family: carry the certified copy of the bail order; prepare the bond forms in advance; bring the surety in person with originals and photocopies; disclose other suretyships honestly, because the declaration is part of the record; and if release is urgent and a surety is unavailable, ask counsel whether the order permits, or can be modified to permit, cash deposit in lieu of surety.</p>
</div>

<h2>Forfeiture and discharge</h2>
<p>A surety's obligation is real. If the accused fails to appear, the court records the ground of forfeiture, issues notice to the accused and the surety, and may recover the bond amount as if it were a fine; a reasoned order can reduce the amount on sufficient cause shown. The obligation is, however, neither perpetual nor irrevocable: a surety who loses confidence in the accused may apply for discharge, upon which the court directs the accused to furnish fresh security for the future. Discharge protects the surety prospectively only — liability already incurred from a past non-appearance survives.</p>

<h2>Why this stage deserves a lawyer's attention</h2>
<p>Bail practice tends to treat everything after the order as clerical. It is not. The choice between one surety and two, the bond amount, a local-surety stipulation, and the availability of cash in lieu are all matters counsel can address in the bail hearing itself, by inviting the court to frame workable conditions — and afterwards, by seeking modification rather than letting an unworkable condition keep a client in custody that the bail order itself says should end.</p>
